From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 13:38:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_sync: Replace the resolving list entry when
 the IRK changed

hci_le_add_resolve_list_sync() returns early when the device already has
an entry in the resolving list, without looking at the key in it. A
device that is paired again gets a new IRK while it is still known to
the kernel: Unpair Device and the new pairing can happen without a
passive scan update in between, so the entry of the old pairing is never
removed. The controller then keeps resolving with the old IRK, the
resolvable private addresses the device uses from then on do not resolve,
its advertisements never pass the accept list and it cannot reconnect
until the controller is powered off and on.

Seen with a Surface Slim Pen 2 that is bonded again in the same boot: it
advertises, an unfiltered scan sees it and resolves its address in the
host, and the background scan never reports it.

Compare the IRK of the existing entry and replace the entry when it
differs.

Signed-off-by: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
---
 net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
index a92a81846..69dd250cb 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
@@ -2504,8 +2504,17 @@ static int hci_le_add_resolve_list_sync(struct hci_dev *hdev,
 	entry = hci_bdaddr_list_lookup_with_irk(&hdev->le_resolv_list,
 						&params->addr,
 						params->addr_type);
-	if (entry)
-		return 0;
+	if (entry) {
+		if (!memcmp(entry->peer_irk, irk->val, 16))
+			return 0;
+
+		/* The device was paired again and has a new IRK. With the old
+		 * one in the controller its resolvable private addresses do
+		 * not resolve and never pass the accept list.
+		 */
+		hci_le_del_resolve_list_sync(hdev, &params->addr,
+					     params->addr_type);
+	}
 
 	cp.bdaddr_type = params->addr_type;
 	bacpy(&cp.bdaddr, &params->addr);
-- 
2.55.0

